WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.351
Thirty kilometres of ice.

2
00:00:02.351 --> 00:00:03.988
That is the length of the Gangotri

3
00:00:03.988 --> 00:00:06.206
glacier, one of the largest in the

4
00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:07.330
Himalayas.

5
00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:09.622
A glacier is a mass of ice

6
00:00:09.622 --> 00:00:12.328
moving slowly downhill because of gravity.

7
00:00:12.328 --> 00:00:14.290
It may move a few centimetres to

8
00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:15.785
a few metres a day.

9
00:00:16.186 --> 00:00:16.883
As it moves,

10
00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:18.311
it scrapes its valley into a wide

11
00:00:18.311 --> 00:00:20.296
U shape, and leaves heaps of rock

12
00:00:20.296 --> 00:00:21.641
and debris behind.

13
00:00:22.041 --> 00:00:22.947
At its snout,

14
00:00:22.947 --> 00:00:25.211
called Gaumukh, the ice melts.

15
00:00:25.211 --> 00:00:26.929
This is where the Bhagirathi,

16
00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:28.194
a headstream of the Ganga,

17
00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:29.261
is born.

18
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.727
Because Himalayan rivers are fed by melting

19
00:00:31.727 --> 00:00:32.783
snow as well as rain,

20
00:00:32.783 --> 00:00:34.292
they flow all year.

21
00:00:34.292 --> 00:00:36.369
Geographers call them perennial.

22
00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:39.021
But Gangotri has been shrinking since seventeen

23
00:00:39.021 --> 00:00:40.403
eighty.

24
00:00:40.403 --> 00:00:42.493
NASA found it retreated more than eight

25
00:00:42.493 --> 00:00:45.893
hundred and fifty metres in twenty-five years.

26
00:00:46.292 --> 00:00:47.778
For GS One:

27
00:00:47.778 --> 00:00:49.949
glaciers are slow rivers of ice,

28
00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.109
and they keep the great Himalayan rivers

29
00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:53.976
flowing through the dry months.

